{"name":"Déjà Vu","hex":"#bed1cc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"déjà vu","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The subjective, unexpected feeling of having experienced something before, especially when that is not the case.","Loosely or humorously, anything one has done before or is being repeated."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To experience déjà vu; to see (something) as though having seen it before."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/d%C3%A9j%C3%A0_vu"}],"entries":[{"title":"Déjà vu","blocks":[{"text":"Déjà vu is the phenomenon of feeling like one has lived through the present situation in the past. It is an illusion of memory whereby—despite a strong sense of recollection—the time, place, and context of the \"previous\" experience are uncertain or impossible. Approximately two-thirds of surveyed populations report experiencing déjà vu at least one time in their lives. The phenomenon manifests occasionally as a symptom of seizure auras, and some researchers have associated chronic or frequent \"pathological\" déjà vu with neurological or psychiatric illness. Experiencing déjà vu has been correlated with higher socioeconomic status, better educational attainment, and lower ages. People who travel often, frequently watch films, or frequently remember their dreams are also more likely to report experiencing déjà vu than others."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9j%C3%A0_vu","wikidata":"Q158103"},{"title":"Déjà Vu (2006 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Déjà Vu is a 2006 American science fiction action film directed by Tony Scott, written by Bill Marsilii and Terry Rossio, and produced by Jerry Bruckheimer. The film stars Denzel Washington, Paula Patton, Jim Caviezel, Val Kilmer, Adam Goldberg and Bruce Greenwood. It involves an ATF agent who travels back in time in an attempt to prevent a domestic terrorist attack that takes place in New Orleans and to save a woman with whom he falls in love."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/cf/DejaVuBigPoster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9j%C3%A0_Vu_(2006_film)","wikidata":"Q252409"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/%C3%89mile_Boirac_2.jpg/330px-%C3%89mile_Boirac_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/%C3%89mile_Boirac_2.jpg/330px-%C3%89mile_Boirac_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Émile Boirac in 1917","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%C3%89mile_Boirac_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Functional_magnetic_resonance_imaging.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Functional_magnetic_resonance_imaging.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"An example of an fMRI brain scan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFunctional_magnetic_resonance_imaging.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Wormhole-demo.png/500px-Wormhole-demo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Wormhole-demo.png/500px-Wormhole-demo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A topological representation almost identical to Greene's idea used in the film to explain a wormhole.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWormhole-demo.png"}],"sounds":{"déjà vu":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-d%C3%A9j%C3%A0_vu.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&ut